          "type": "turn",
          "question": "If someone has a raised blood pressure reading but no diagnosis of hypertension, which chapter does that belong in?",
          "answer": "A raised blood pressure reading with no diagnosis of hypertension belongs in **Chapter 18, \"Symptoms, signs and abnormal clinical and laboratory findings\" (R00–R99)**. The retrieved coding summary places it in the R00–R09 block, which covers symptoms and signs of the circulatory and respiratory systems. The passages I retrieved do not print the code number R03.0 itself, so I can confirm the block but not that exact code.\n\nIt is not filed under circulatory disease because the circulatory chapter (I00–I99) is for recorded diseases. The hypertension code, I10, is used only once a diagnosis of hypertension has been documented. The coding summary says a single raised reading, or a series of readings, without that diagnosis \"is a finding rather than a disease.\" The record's diagnosis decides the chapter, not the number itself.\n\nHypertension is a diagnosis based on repeated measurements over time, and a single high reading is not enough on its own. Whether a reading means anything for a particular person is a question for their clinician.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
